  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 302 AM EDT TUE OCT 6 2026\n\nThe period is expected to begin with an upper level ridge centered \noff the southwest U.S coast, with an upper low centered in the \nHudson Bay vicinity and a trough extending into the Upper MS Valley \nand parts of the Great Lakes. At the surface, a ridge of high \npressure should extend from the Southern Appalachians into the \nAtlantic, while a sfc front in advance of troughing extending into \nthe Upper MS Valley and Great Lakes should extend into northern MI \nto the Central Plains. Meanwhile, a lingering frontal zone should \nextend across portions of the Gulf. \n\nWednesday to Thursday night, the upper low in Canada is progged to \nbecome centered in portions of Quebec, while the associated \nshortwave trough moves across the Great Lakes Wednesday and \nWednesday night, and across portions of the OH Valley on \nThursday. Further west, upper ridging should become centered in \nthe Pacific to the west and southwest of Baja, with ridging \ncontinuing to extend into the western CONUS. Downstream, by late \nThursday night, a 500-mb trough axis is anticipated from Quebec \ninto the eastern Great Lakes to Central to Southern Appalachians. \nGuidance generally also has a weak upper low in or near TX by \nThursday night and guidance (which now includes the GFS, AI-GFS, \nECMWF, and AI-ECMWF and many of their ensembles) also points \ntoward possible tropical system formation potentially as early as \nthis time frame or at least a sfc low somewhere in the central to \nsouthwestern Gulf. Sfc low pressure in advance of the upper low in\nCanada and its associated shortwave should move across the Great \nLakes and Northeast, but the southern end will likely become ill- \ndefined or diffuse as it approaches the OH Valley. A ridge of sfc \nhigh pressure should generally remain through this period in the \nsouthern to central Appalachians with a trend of 500 mb height \nrises over the northern portions of the Gulf states into the TN \nValley and Southern Appalachians by later Thursday night.\n\nFor eastern KY, the pattern should favor a moderation in high \ntemperatures beginning Wednesday, and a ridge/valley temperature \nsplit for Wednesday night and probably Thursday night as well. \nOvernight lows should tend to moderate as sfc dewpoints inch \nupward compared to earlier in the week, but valley fog should \ncontinue to be a feature each night with ridging generally \ndominating. The weakening front and passing shortwave could lead \nto a bit more cloud cover later Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning than currently reflected in sky grids.\n\nFriday to Saturday night, a narrow upper ridge axis that shifts \nnorth is progged by recent GFS and ECMWF runs to be situated from\nthe Carolinas across the southern Appalachians to near the \nconfluence of the OH and TN Rivers for Friday to Friday night \nwhile an upper low becomes centered in the Arklatex vicinity by \nlater Friday night or early Saturday. At that point, moisture \nshould begin to or have spread to near the Gulf Coast or into the \nGulf coast states and eventually north into the Southern \nAppalachians and TN Valley and possibly into the Lower OH Valley \nby later Saturday or Saturday night pending the evolution of a\npotential tropical system in the Gulf, while the upper low may \nmove to near the mid MS Valley vicinity by late Saturday night. \nGuidance has uncertainty with the strength of this system with the\n12Z operational GFS run stronger than the 06Z GFS run or the last\ntwo operational ECMWF runs. Additional 18z and 00z continue to\nincrease confidence in a potential tropical system and parent low\nmoving north or northeast toward, and possibly through, the TN \nand OH Valley regions. It should be pointed out, however, that \neven though guidance is showing this as an increasing possibility,\nexperience and wisdom suggests waiting to see when or even if an \norganized system actually develops in the Gulf within the expected\ntime frame and its potential feedback with the developing parent\nlow. However given recent model trends would expect the current \n~45 to 50% PoPs arriving Saturday night into Sunday may increase \nto near or above 60% with the next forecast package. \n\nBy Monday, much of the guidance has the parent low and much of \nthe moisture tracking east of the area, with upper ridging and \nheight rises around or near the end of the period just upstream\nover the MS Valley.",
